<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yonne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yonne is a river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, about 290 km long, rising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morvan">Morvan</a> and joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> at Montereau. It flows past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxerre">Auxerre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sens">Sens</a> and gives its name to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. In antiquity its valley was the land of the Senones.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yonne-river.jpg" alt="Yonne" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Own work · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Auxerre_-_111.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
